<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Panama Has Delicious Food! – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1 of 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Planning Meals for My Family</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Panama Has Delicious Food!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow narratives involving familiar contexts. Interactive: Respond to questions on familiar and routine matters.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow narratives related to types of food (e.g., "Vegetables and grains are healthy."). Can respond to questions about healthy eating (e.g., "What types of food are good for you?" "Fruits and vegetables are good choices."). Can summarize information about meal planning shared in class (e.g., "Use a chart or a Venn diagram to present the main ideas shared in class.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and displays a large, colorful poster or flashcards of various Panamanian foods (e.g., rice, beans, mangos, watermelon, chicken, fish, bread). The teacher points to each picture and says, "Look! What is this? This is delicious food!" The teacher asks, "Do you like [pointing to rice] rice?" and encourages choral repetition for key new vocabulary: <i>rice, beans, bread, mangos, watermelon, vegetables, fruits</i>. This connects to the theme "Panama Has Delicious Food!".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of a mango and says, "This is a mango. It's a fruit." The teacher then holds up a picture of lettuce and says, "This is lettuce. It's a vegetable." The teacher clearly pronounces /f/ in "fruit" and /v/ in "vegetables," encouraging students to listen carefully. The teacher models simple sentences like, "Fruits are healthy." and "I like some bread for breakfast." The teacher introduces the concepts of <i>breakfast, lunch, dinner, snack</i> by showing pictures of different meals.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses simple questions to check understanding of the new vocabulary.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a tomato a fruit or a vegetable?" (Expected: "A vegetable!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we eat dinner in the morning or at night?" (Expected: "At night!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we drink water?" (Expected: "Yes!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Planning Meals for My Family")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio narrative about a Panamanian family (e.g., "The Garcia Family") discussing their weekly meal plan. The audio is designed at A1.3 level, featuring simple sentences using present simple, 'can', countable/uncountable nouns, and target vocabulary (breakfast, lunch, dinner, snack, rice, beans, bread, water, juice, mangos, watermelon, vegetables, fruits, cook, eat). The teacher prepares a worksheet with pictures of different food items and simple true/false statements.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow narratives involving familiar contexts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Listening:</b> The teacher plays the audio once. Students listen to understand the main idea. The teacher asks, "What is the family talking about? Is it about toys, school, or food?" (Expected: "Food!"). "Are they planning a party or a meal?" (Expected: "A meal!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Listening:</b> The teacher plays the audio a second time. Students listen for specific details. On their worksheet, they mark 'True' or 'False' for statements like: "The family eats rice for breakfast." "They can cook beans for lunch." "Mom likes mangos."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Pictures):</b> The teacher plays the audio a third time. Students listen and point to or circle the food items mentioned for each meal on their worksheet, which contains pictures of different foods. E.g., "For breakfast, they like bread and juice." Students circle the pictures of bread and juice.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Present simple (e.g., "Fruits and vegetables are delicious and healthy."), modal verb 'can', countable/uncountable nouns (e.g., "some rice"), vocabulary (breakfast, lunch, dinner, snack, rice, beans, bread, water, juice, mangos, watermelon, tomatoes, lettuce, fruits, vegetables, cook, eat, enjoy), pronunciation of /v/ in "vegetables" and /f/ in "fruit", phonemic awareness (-ing in "cooking").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listening Cloze Activity:</b> The teacher distributes a transcript of the audio narrative from Stage 2 with key target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>rice, beans, juice, cook, healthy, mangos</i>) removed. Students listen to the audio again (played twice) and fill in the missing words on their transcript. The teacher then displays the correct answers for students to check their work.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Draw/Select:</b> The teacher plays short, distinct sentences or phrases related to food preferences and meal ideas. Students listen and either draw the food item mentioned or select it from a provided set of pictures on their worksheet. Examples: "I like to eat some bread for breakfast." (Students draw bread or circle a picture of bread). "We can cook vegetables." (Students draw vegetables or circle a picture of vegetables). The teacher specifically uses sentences that highlight /v/ and /f/ sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Listen and Classify (Healthy/Unhealthy):</b> The teacher plays a list of food items one by one (e.g., "fruit," "candy," "vegetables," "soda," "water," "fried chicken"). Students listen and classify each item as "Healthy" or "Not Healthy" in two columns on their worksheet. Afterwards, students briefly compare their answers with a partner, saying "Fruit is healthy" or "Candy is not healthy," reinforcing the present simple and vocabulary through guided speaking based on listening.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Planning Meals for My Family")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Guided pair interaction – "Meal Suggestion Chain."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs. The teacher first models the activity: "For lunch, I like rice and beans. What about you?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A starts by naming one food item they like for a specific meal (e.g., "For breakfast, I like some bread.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B listens carefully to Student A's suggestion. Then, Student B responds by asking a question about a different food preference or suggesting an additional item using "Can we...?" or "We can eat..." (e.g., "Can we eat some fruit too?" or "We can eat some mangos.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A then listens to Student B's question/suggestion and responds, agreeing or disagreeing, and then suggests another food or meal idea. They continue this chain for a few rounds, focusing on active listening and appropriate responses.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, providing support and noting language use, especially the use of present simple, 'can', countable/uncountable nouns, and pronunciation of /f/ and /v/.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> This activity directly simulates "Planning Meals for My Family" by having students verbally exchange ideas about food preferences, incorporating the "Panama Has Delicious Food!" theme through the use of local food examples (mangos, rice, beans).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during the "Meal Suggestion Chain" activity and a short "Listen and Respond" exit ticket.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Teacher Observation:</b> The teacher uses a checklist to observe students' ability to listen and respond using target vocabulary and grammar during the pair work.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Exit Ticket (Listen and Respond):</b> The teacher says 2-3 short questions about food preferences or meal items (e.g., "What do you like to eat for lunch?" "Are mangos fruits or vegetables?"). Students write a short answer on a small paper slip before leaving.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target vocabulary (e.g., /f/ in "fruit," /v/ in "vegetables").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., naming different meals, food items).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of understanding through correct responses to spoken questions/suggestions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to respond to questions on familiar and routine matters.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket Prompts:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new food words did I hear and understand today?" (Students write 2-3 words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I understand when someone talks about breakfast, lunch, and dinner?" (Students circle "Yes" or "No" and give an example).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b> (To be completed after the lesson or as homework)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of listening about food was easy for me today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part was a little difficult, and how can I practice more?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What worked well in engaging students and developing their listening skills for this topic? The visual aids and repetitive listening tasks seemed effective."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next lesson? Some students struggled with differentiating /f/ and /v/ sounds; I need to include more targeted pronunciation practice in the next lesson's warm-up or during reading."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson (Lesson 2 – Reading Comprehension):</b> "In our next lesson, we will *read* a short text about traditional Panamanian foods and meal times, using many of the same vocabulary words we heard and practiced today. We will focus on understanding the text and finding specific information."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">2-3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>